 # Transfer Function Optimization and Learning
 
-Also called: Automatic Transfer Function Design, Data-Driven TF Generation
+Also called: Automatic Transfer Function Design, Differentiable Volume Rendering for TF Optimization, Data-Driven TF Generation
 
 ## Overview
-Transfer Function Optimization and Learning automates the creation of classification functions by adjusting parameters to match a target visual appearance or perceptual goal, rather than relying on manual user specification. It solves the labor-intensive and subjective nature of manual TF design, particularly for complex or large datasets. This family is distinguished from [Histogram-Based Transfer Function Generation](6182-histogram-based-transfer-function-generation.md) by optimizing based on rendered output or perceptual metrics rather than solely on data distribution statistics, and from [Default 3D Transfer Function](5647-default-3d-transfer-function.md) by its adaptive, iterative nature.
+This family of methods automatically derives volume rendering transfer functions (TFs) by optimizing parameters to match target visual appearances, visibility metrics, or user intents, rather than relying on manual user interaction. It solves the labor-intensive and subjective process of manually tuning opacity and color mappings for complex volumetric data. What sets this family apart from its siblings (such as histogram-based or clustering-based generation) is the use of iterative optimization loops—often involving gradient descent, simplex methods, or neural networks—to minimize a specific loss function (e.g., image reconstruction error, visibility discrepancy) between the rendered output and a target state [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
